Given vectors are $\vec{AB}=3\hat{i}+\hat{j}-\hat{k}$ and $\vec{AC}=\hat{i}-\hat{j}+3\hat{k}$.
Since P is equidistant from lines AB and AC, P lies on the angle bisector of $\angle BAC$. Let $2\theta$ be the angle between $\vec{AB}$ and $\vec{AC}$. Then the angle between $\vec{AP}$ and $\vec{AB}$ is $\theta$.
First, calculate the cosine of the angle $2\theta$ between $\vec{AB}$ and $\vec{AC}$:
$\cos$(2$\theta$) = $\frac${$\vec{AB}$ \cdot $\vec{AC}$}{|$\vec{AB}$||$\vec{AC}$|}
$\vec{AB}$ \cdot $\vec{AC}$ = (3)(1) + (1)(-1) + (-1)(3) = 3 - 1 - 3 = -1
|$\vec{AB}$| = $\sqrt{$3^2 + 1^2$ + (-1)^2} = \sqrt{9+1+1} = \sqrt{11}$
|$\vec{AC}$| = $\sqrt{$1^2$ + (-1)^2 + $3^2$} = \sqrt{1+1+9} = \sqrt{11} \cos$(2$\theta$) = $\frac{-1}${$\sqrt{11}$ \cdot $\sqrt{11}$} = -$\frac{1}{11}$
Now, use the double angle identity $\cos(2\theta) = 1 - 2\sin^2\theta$ to find $\sin\theta$:
1 - 2$\$sin^2$$$\theta$ = -$\frac{1}{11}$
2$\$sin^2$$$\theta$ = 1 + $\frac{1}{11} = \frac{12}{11} \$sin^2$$$\theta = \frac{6}{11} \sin \theta = \sqrt${$\frac{6}{11}$}
The area of triangle ABP is given by $\frac{1}{2} |\vec{AB}| |\vec{AP}| \sin\theta$.
We are given $|\vec{AP}| = \frac{\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and we found $|\vec{AB}| = \sqrt{11}$ and $\sin\theta = \sqrt{\frac{6}{11}}$.
$\text{Area}$($\triangle$ APB) = $\frac{1}{2}$ \times $\sqrt{11}$ \times $\frac${$\sqrt{5}$}{2} \times $\sqrt${$\frac{6}{11}$}
$\text{Area}$($\triangle$ APB) = $\frac{1}{2}$ \times $\frac${$\sqrt{11}$ \times $\sqrt{5}$ \times $\sqrt{6}$}{2 \times $\sqrt{11}$}
$\text{Area}$($\triangle$ APB) = $\frac{1}{4}$ \times $\sqrt{5 \times 6} \text{Area}$($\triangle$ APB) = $\frac${$\sqrt{30}$}{4}
The area of the triangle ABP is $\frac{\sqrt{30}}{4}$.

questions.question:
Let $\vec{a} = 9\hat{i} - 13\hat{j} + 25\hat{k}$, $\vec{b} = 3\hat{i} + 7\hat{j} - 13\hat{k}$ and $\vec{c} = 17\hat{i} - 2\hat{j} + \hat{k}$ be three given vectors. If $\vec{r}$ is vector such that $\vec{r} \times \vec{a} = (\vec{b} + \vec{c}) \times \vec{a}$ and $\vec{r} \cdot (\vec{b} - \vec{c}) = 0$, then
$\frac${|593$\vec{r}$ + 67$\vec{a}$|^2}{(593)^2}
is equal to _______.

questions.explanation:
### Step 1: Use the given vector cross product condition.

$\vec{r}$ \times $\vec{a} = \vec{c}$ \times $\vec{a} \Rightarrow \vec{r}$ \times $\vec{a} - \vec{c}$ \times $\vec{a}$ = 0 $\Rightarrow$ ($\vec{r} - \vec{c}$) \times $\vec{a}$ = 0 This implies that the vector $(\vec{r} - \vec{c})$ is parallel to $\vec{a}$. Therefore, we can write: $\vec{r} - \vec{c} = \lambda \vec{a} \Rightarrow \vec{r} = \vec{c} + \lambda \vec{a}$

### Step 2: Use the given vector dot product condition to find $\lambda$.

Substitute the expression for $\vec{r}$ into the second condition $\vec{r} \cdot \vec{b} = 0$: ($\vec{c} + \lambda \vec{a}$) \cdot $\vec{b}$ = 0 $\Rightarrow \vec{c}$ \cdot $\vec{b} + \lambda$ ($\vec{a}$ \cdot $\vec{b}$) = 0 Given vectors are: $\vec{a}$ = 2$\hat{i}$ - 7$\hat{j}$ + 5$\hat{k} \vec{b} = \hat{i} + \hat{k} \vec{c} = \hat{i}$ + 2$\hat{j}$ - 3$\hat{k}$ Calculate the dot products $\vec{c} \cdot \vec{b}$ and $\vec{a} \cdot \vec{b}$: $\vec{c}$ \cdot $\vec{b}$ = (1)(1) + (2)(0) + (-3)(1) = 1 - 0 - 3 = -2 $\vec{a}$ \cdot $\vec{b}$ = (2)(1) + (-7)(0) + (5)(1) = 2 + 0 + 5 = 7 Substitute these values back into the equation $\vec{c} \cdot \vec{b} + \lambda (\vec{a} \cdot \vec{b}) = 0$: -2 + $\lambda$ (7) = 0 7$\lambda$ = 2 $\lambda = \frac{2}{7}$

### Step 3: Find the vector $\vec{r}$.

Now substitute the value of $\lambda$ back into the expression for $\vec{r}$: $\vec{r} = \vec{c} + \frac{2}{7} \vec{a} \vec{r}$ = ($\hat{i}$ + 2$\hat{j}$ - 3$\hat{k}$) + $\frac{2}{7}$(2$\hat{i}$ - 7$\hat{j}$ + 5$\hat{k}$) $\vec{r} = \hat{i}$ + 2$\hat{j}$ - 3$\hat{k} + \frac{4}{7} \hat{i} - \frac{14}{7} \hat{j} + \frac{10}{7} \hat{k} \vec{r} = \hat{i}$ + 2$\hat{j}$ - 3$\hat{k} + \frac{4}{7} \hat{i}$ - 2$\hat{j} + \frac{10}{7} \hat{k} \vec{r} = \left(1 + \frac{4}{7}\right) \hat{i}$ + (2 - 2)$\hat{j} + \left(-3 + \frac{10}{7}\right) \hat{k} \vec{r} = \left(\frac{7+4}{7}\right) \hat{i}$ + 0$\hat{j} + \left(\frac{-21+10}{7}\right) \hat{k} \vec{r} = \frac{11}{7} \hat{i} - \frac{11}{7} \hat{k}$

### Step 4: Calculate the magnitude of $\vec{r}$.

The magnitude of $\vec{r}$ is given by:
|$\vec{r}$| =$ \sqrt{\left(\frac{11}{7}\right)$^2 + $\left(-\frac{11}{7}\right)$^2 + (0)^2}
|$\vec{r}$| = $\sqrt${$\frac{121}{49} + \frac{121}{49}$}
|$\vec{r}$| = $\sqrt${2 \times $\frac{121}{49}$}
|$\vec{r}$| = $\frac${$\sqrt{2}$ \times $\sqrt{121}$}{$\sqrt{49}$}
|$\vec{r}$| = $\frac${$\sqrt{2}$ \times 11}{7}
|$\vec{r}$| = $\frac${11$\sqrt{2}$}{7}
The magnitude of $\vec{r}$ is $\frac{11\sqrt{2}}{7}$.

Given points:
• $O(0, 0, 0)$
• $P(1, 0, 0)$
• $Q(0, 1, 0)$
• $R(0, 0, 1)$
• $S \left$($\frac{1}{2}$, $\frac{1}{2}$, $\frac{1}{2} \right$) 
• $T(1, 1, 1)$
Now, we calculate the vectors $\vec{p}, \vec{q}, \vec{r}, \vec{t}$:
$\vec{p} = \vec{SP}$ = P - S = $\left(1 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{i} + \left(0 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{j} + \left(0 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{k} = \frac{1}{2} \hat{i} - \frac{1}{2} \hat{j} - \frac{1}{2} \hat{k} = \frac${$\hat{i} - \hat{j} - \hat{k}$}{2}
$\vec{q} = \vec{SQ}$ = Q - S = $\left(0 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{i} + \left(1 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{j} + \left(0 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{k}$ = -$\frac{1}{2} \hat{i} + \frac{1}{2} \hat{j} - \frac{1}{2} \hat{k} = \frac${-$\hat{i} + \hat{j} - \hat{k}$}{2}
$\vec{r} = \vec{SR}$ = R - S = $\left(0 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{i} + \left(0 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{j} + \left(1 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{k}$ = -$\frac{1}{2} \hat{i} - \frac{1}{2} \hat{j} + \frac{1}{2} \hat{k} = \frac${-$\hat{i} - \hat{j} + \hat{k}$}{2}
$\vec{t} = \vec{ST}$ = T - S = $\left(1 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{i} + \left(1 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{j} + \left(1 - \frac{1}{2}\right) \hat{k} = \frac{1}{2} \hat{i} + \frac{1}{2} \hat{j} + \frac{1}{2} \hat{k} = \frac${$\hat{i} + \hat{j} + \hat{k}$}{2}
Now, calculate the cross product $\vec{p} \times \vec{q}$:
$\vec{p}$ \times $\vec{q} = \frac{1}{4} \begin{vmatrix} \hat{i}$ & $\hat{j}$ & $\hat{k}$ \\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\ -1 & 1 & -1 $\end{vmatrix} = \frac{1}{4}$ [$\hat{i}$(1 - (-1)) - $\hat{j}$(-1 - 1) + $\hat{k}$(1 - 1)] = $\frac{1}{4}$ [2$\hat{i}$ + 2$\hat{j}$] = $\frac${2($\hat{i} + \hat{j}$)}{4} = $\frac${$\hat{i} + \hat{j}$}{2}
Next, calculate the cross product $\vec{r} \times \vec{t}$:
$\vec{r}$ \times $\vec{t} = \frac{1}{4} \begin{vmatrix} \hat{i}$ & $\hat{j}$ & $\hat{k}$ \\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 $\end{vmatrix} = \frac{1}{4}$ [$\hat{i}$(-1 - 1) - $\hat{j}$(-1 - 1) + $\hat{k}$(-1 - (-1))] = $\frac{1}{4}$ [-2$\hat{i}$ + 2$\hat{j}$] = $\frac${2(-$\hat{i} + \hat{j}$)}{4} = $\frac${-$\hat{i} + \hat{j}$}{2}
Finally, calculate the cross product of the results and its magnitude:
($\vec{p}$ \times $\vec{q}$) \times ($\vec{r}$ \times $\vec{t}$) = $\frac{1}{4} \begin{vmatrix} \hat{i}$ & $\hat{j}$ & $\hat{k}$ \\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\ -1 & 1 & 0 $\end{vmatrix} = \frac{1}{4}$ [$\hat{i}$(0 - 0) - $\hat{j}$(0 - 0) + $\hat{k}$(1 - (-1))] = $\frac{1}{4}$ [2$\hat{k}$] = $\frac${2$\hat{k}$}{4} = $\frac${$\hat{k}$}{2}
|($\vec{p}$ \times $\vec{q}$) \times ($\vec{r}$ \times $\vec{t}$)| = $\left|\frac{\hat{k}}{2}\right| = \frac{1}{2}$ = 0.5
The value of $|(\vec{p} \times \vec{q}) \times (\vec{r} \times \vec{t})|$ is 0.5.
